/T (There is a pattern in such instances. Exchange exists, but it is not organised through markets. It is embedded within social arrangements by the ways of territory, hierarchy, kinship, and obligation. Production and distribution are not separate economic processes, they are regulated through norms, authority, and collective structures that define who can access what, and under what conditions. Also the mere fact, that a tribe which used money differentiated it very little economically from other tribes on the same cultural level, who did not, shows how markets and their regulation through prices is constructed and is definately not a natural extension of human exchange, which is embedded in social relations and reciprocation.)
/E (There is a pattern in such instances. Exchange exists, but it is not organised through markets. It is embedded within social arrangements by the ways of territory, hierarchy, kinship, and obligation. Production and distribution are not separate economic processes, they are regulated through norms, authority, and collective structures that define who can access what, and under what conditions. Also the mere fact, that a tribe which used money differentiated it very little economically from other tribes on the same cultural level, who did not, shows how markets and their regulation through prices is constructed and is definately not a natural extension of human exchange, which is embedded in social relations and reciprocation.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
