>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Here, individuals are embedded within structures that shape both their options and their participation. Preferences are not expressed, not even once, they are outright formed within contexts of exclusion, adaptation, and constraint. The model can describe transactions, but it struggles to account for the conditions that turns those transactions into necessity.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (At the same time, these systems cannot be reduced to just exploitation alone, nor can they be romanticised as alternatives. They function as mechanisms of survival where formal economic structures fail to provide inclusion. They sustain life, even as they reproduce constraints.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Such marketization of needs and constraints demonstrate that economic activity can be organised through relationships rather than anonymous exchange, and that participation can be structured by necessity rather than choice. They show that markets, as described by standard economic models, are not always the primary or sufficient framework for understanding how people sustain themselves. These economies do not contradict economic theory, they simply expose its limits. They reveal the boundaries of a model that assumes independence, neutrality, and voluntary exchange by presenting forms of economic life where these conditions do not fully hold.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
