1075 0 obj
<<
/Title (This changes how exchange is understood. In conventional economic theory, exchange is assumed to be voluntary and mutually beneficial, which occur between independent individuals.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1058 0 R
/Prev 1066 0 R
/Next 1088 0 R
>>
endobj
1076 0 obj
<<
/Title (In Kamathipura, transactions occur within asymmetries which exist due to necessity and dependence. In the hijra and khusra economies, exchange is mediated by cultural legitimacy and social obligation rather than purely individual negotiation.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1058 0 R
/Prev 1088 0 R
/Next 1067 0 R
>>
endobj
1077 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1064 0 R
/Prev 1069 0 R
/Next 1089 0 R
>>
endobj
1078 0 obj
<<
/Title (Across these pieces, we move to moments before the market, as we now know it, emerged. Where exchange does not come from agreements between equals but rather from relations which are structured through obligation, access, and power. What appears to us as participation, reveals itself as something which was already mediated and diffused, and then further sustained through systems that no longer need to announce themselves. Within the household, reciprocity holds through expectations instead of balance, and those expectations are internalised, repeated and rarely recognised. Then we look at economies that sit at the margins, where belonging becomes necessary for survival and refusal carries hight cost. Even here exchange continues, but under conditions which violate voluntariness. In the present, as transactions are increasingly replacing continuity, what remains  of exchange is its reorganisation into forms that no longer sustain what they depend on.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1064 0 R
/Prev 1089 0 R
/Next 1070 0 R
>>
endobj
