1132 0 obj
<<
/Title (As Karl Polanyi argues in The Great Transformation, the idea that economic life originates in isolated individuals engaging in exchange, is in fact historically unfounded. Economic systems, for most of the human history, were organised through reciprocity, redistribution, and householding and not through markets, and certainly not through self maximising individuals. Exchange did exist, but it did not give rise to markets, nor did it produce individualism. .)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1091 0 R
/Prev 1125 0 R
/Next 1133 0 R
>>
endobj
1133 0 obj
<<
/Title (As Polanyi makes it clear, the economy was always embedded within social relations, not separate from them. This distinction is not minor. It is profound)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1091 0 R
/Prev 1132 0 R
/Next 1126 0 R
>>
endobj
1134 0 obj
<<
/Title (This confidence with which we assume that life can be calculated is not accidental. It is built on the belief that we understand what we are doing, that we are acting, choosing, deciding, but that confidence rests on a misrecognition, of where decisions come from and of what holds them in place.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1127 0 R
/Next 1137 0 R
>>
endobj
1135 0 obj
<<
/Title (Exchange was reorganised  into something that could be governed by markets.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1138 0 R
/Next 1128 0 R
>>
endobj
1136 0 obj
<<
/Title (Avani Chhahira)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1128 0 R
>>
endobj
1137 0 obj
<<
/Title (What appears to us as a moment of decision, is merely a moment of visibility, of something which was already in motion.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1134 0 R
/Next 1139 0 R
>>
endobj
1138 0 obj
<<
/Title (Exchange does not create relationships, it is created by them.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1140 0 R
/Next 1135 0 R
>>
endobj
1139 0 obj
<<
/Title (Before The Markets)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1137 0 R
/First 1141 0 R
/Last 1141 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1142 0 R
>>
endobj
1140 0 obj
<<
/Title (If early systems of trade and exchange were structured through power, obligation, and embedded relationships, then the figure of the independent individual engaging in voluntary trade cannot be taken as the starting point. It is a much later development, which requires the separation of economic activity from social context.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1143 0 R
/Next 1138 0 R
>>
endobj
1141 0 obj
<<
/Title (Hameed Rehman)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1139 0 R
/First 1144 0 R
/Last 1145 0 R
/Count -8
>>
endobj
1142 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1139 0 R
/Next 1146 0 R
>>
endobj
1143 0 obj
<<
/Title (There is a pattern in such instances. Exchange exists, but it is not organised through markets. It is embedded within social arrangements by the ways of territory, hierarchy, kinship, and obligation. Production and distribution are not separate economic processes, they are regulated through norms, authority, and collective structures that define who can access what, and under what conditions. Also the mere fact, that a tribe which used money differentiated it very little economically from other tribes on the same cultural level, who did not, shows how markets and their regulation through prices is constructed and is definately not a natural extension of human exchange, which is embedded in social relations and reciprocation.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1146 0 R
/Next 1140 0 R
>>
endobj
1144 0 obj
<<
/Title (Economic exchange is often imagined to begin with a rather simple scene: two individuals, each possessing something the other wants, arriving at an agreement through negotiation. From this, it is assumed, markets emerge naturally due scarcity and human needs.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Next 1147 0 R
>>
endobj
1145 0 obj
<<
/Title (Participation in this system was not freely open. It required entry into a network of recognised partners, often maintained over long periods. Giving here was not a discretionary act in the everyday sense, it created a binding expectation of return. That was exchange.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1148 0 R
>>
endobj
1146 0 obj
<<
/Title (This is a form of exchange which is governed by continuity and is not driven by the incentive of immediate gains. Objects circulate, for maintaining the relationship itself. Value lies in participation and recognition, as much as the intrinsic value of the object being exchanged. The system regulates behaviour through expectation, status, and the possibility of exclusion.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1142 0 R
/Next 1143 0 R
>>
endobj
1147 0 obj
<<
/Title (However, history suggests a very different starting point: one in which exchange was embedded within social structures and organised through power, obligation, and control.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1144 0 R
/Next 1149 0 R
>>
endobj
1148 0 obj
<<
/Title (These objects were not accumulated or consumed in the conventional sense, because  their movement itself carried value, which was deciphered through their history, their prior holders, and the position of those who possessed them.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1150 0 R
/Next 1145 0 R
>>
endobj
1149 0 obj
<<
/Title (In Central Australian communities such as the Dieri, the acquisition of goods like red ochre, which is used for ceremonial and symbolic purposes, required long-distance expeditions to regions where it was naturally available. Infact, it was such long-distance trade, or rather external trade, which came to being first, and then further gave rise to internal trade.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1147 0 R
/Next 1151 0 R
>>
endobj
1150 0 obj
<<
/Title (A different but equally structured form of exchange can be observed in the Kula ring of the Trobriand Islands. In this system, shell necklaces \(soulava\) and armbands \(mwali\)  was traded across a network of islands in fixed directional patterns.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1152 0 R
/Next 1148 0 R
>>
endobj
1151 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1141 0 R
/Prev 1149 0 R
/Next 1152 0 R
>>
endobj
